$macro.generated_by(self::path)

=[ Quick Start ]

This guide walks you through creating, checking, exporting, and serving your first Tomet document in ten minutes.

==[ 1. Install ]

$include(addr(tmt:tmtroot/docs/install_tools.tmt#quick-install), heading: false)

For other installation methods, see @link[Install Tomet and Sisho](tmt:tmtroot/docs/install_tools.tmt).

==[ 2. Write your first document ]

Create a file named `hello.tmt` containing headings, a list with an ordered sub-item, a callout, and a table:

$raw(tmt("tmtroot/examples/hello.tmt")::source, lang: "tmt")

You now have a complete Tomet document in a single file.

==[ 3. Check your document ]

Run `sisho check .` to validate document syntax and check formatting rules across the directory:

```bash
`` sisho check .
```

```
`` sisho check .
`` tomet 0.5.0, twrit 0.4.0, catasto 0.1.0
``
`` ✓ tomet check (0.02s)
`` ✗ tomet format --check (0.01s): exit 1
``     ./hello.tmt
``     1 of 1 file(s) not formatted
`` ✓ tomet export --check (0.02s)
`` ✓ twrit check (0.02s)
`` ✓ catasto check (0.03s)
``
`` 1 of 5 steps did not pass
```

The document parsed and validated cleanly, but failed the formatting check because hand-written spacing differed from canonical style.

Run `sisho fix .` to automatically format all files in place:

```bash
`` sisho fix .
```

```
`` sisho fix .
`` tomet 0.5.0, twrit 0.4.0, catasto 0.1.0
``
`` ✓ twrit check --fix (0.00s)
`` ✓ tomet export (0.01s)
`` ✓ tomet format -i (0.00s)
``
`` all 3 steps passed
```

`sisho fix` reformatted `hello.tmt` to match canonical indentation and table alignment.

Now run `sisho check .` again to confirm that everything passes:

```bash
`` sisho check .
```

```
`` sisho check .
`` tomet 0.5.0, twrit 0.4.0, catasto 0.1.0
``
`` ✓ tomet check (0.01s)
`` ✓ tomet format --check (0.01s)
`` ✓ tomet export --check (0.01s)
`` ✓ twrit check (0.00s)
`` ✓ catasto check (0.01s)
``
`` all 5 steps passed
```

All five verification steps now pass.

==[ 4. Export to Markdown and HTML ]

Add an `::export` declaration to the top of `hello.tmt` to define the target formats and output paths:

```tmt
`` ::export(
``   type: (commonmark, html)
``   path: ( commonmark: "hello.md", html: "hello.html" )
`` )
```

Run `tomet export hello.tmt` to generate the output files:

```bash
`` tomet export hello.tmt
```

```
`` Exported hello.tmt -> ./hello.md
`` Exported hello.tmt -> ./hello.html
```

The exporter generated `hello.md` in CommonMark format:

```markdown
`` # Hello, Tomet
``
`` Tomet is a *structured* markup language.
``
`` - a list item
``   1. an ordered child
``
`` > [!note]
`` > A callout.
``
`` | name | kind |
`` | --- | --- |
`` | tomet | language |
```

And `hello.html` as a standalone HTML page:

```html
`` <!DOCTYPE html>
`` <html lang="ja">
`` <head>
`` <meta charset="utf-8">
`` <title>hello</title>
`` ...
```

==[ 5. Preview with live reload ]

Run `sisho serve hello.tmt` to launch a local development server:

```bash
`` sisho serve hello.tmt
```

```
`` Serving at http://127.0.0.1:8787 (Ctrl+C to stop)
```

The server re-renders the document on every HTTP request so edits appear on browser reload.

Fetch the page with `curl` to confirm it is serving:

```bash
`` curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8787 | head -n 5
```

```html
`` <!DOCTYPE html>
`` <html lang="ja">
`` <head>
`` <meta charset="utf-8">
`` <title>hello</title>
```

Stop the server with `Ctrl+C` when finished.

==[ 6. Import an existing Markdown file ]

Use `sisho convert` to migrate an existing Markdown document into Tomet:

```bash
`` sisho convert notes.md --from md -o notes.tmt
```

```
`` Converted notes.md -> notes.tmt
```

`sisho convert` translated the Markdown headings, lists, and inline styles into Tomet markup.

Run `sisho fix .` or `tomet format notes.tmt` to produce clean canonical output:

```tmt
`` =[Notes]
``
`` A quick note in Markdown.
```

==[ 7. Configure your editor ]

Install an editor extension to get syntax highlighting, formatting, and real-time diagnostics while editing `.tmt` files.

See the @link[Editors section](tmt:tmtroot/docs/install_tools.tmt#editors) of the install page for setup details covering VS Code, Zed, Helix, and Neovim.

==[ 8. Manage a project with vault.config.tmt ]

Add a `vault.config.tmt` file to your project root to manage multiple Tomet documents as a unified vault.

A vault configuration enables shared formatting preferences, custom metadata fields, schema blueprints, and cross-document link checking with `tomet check-links`:

```bash
`` tomet check-links
```

```
`` 3 file(s) scanned, 0 link(s) checked, 0 broken
```

For more details on vault settings and cross-document expressions, see the @link[Vault Configuration specification](tmt:tmtroot/spec/features/config.tmt).

